  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tunisia: Enhancing Gender Responsiveness in the Justice and Security Sectors</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) Justice and security providers handle GBV cases through a coordinated and survivor-centred approach. 2) Members of Parliament and Ministry of Justice officials develop gender-responsive legislation. 3) An increased number of GBV survivors and women facing violence are aware of and understand their rights and how to access legal, medical, and support services. 4) Legal aid providers use the digital legal aid mechanism to support GBV survivors and women facing violence in exercising their rights. 5) There is an increased participation of and number of leadership roles for women judges in the Judiciary.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Direct beneficiaries: Ministry of Justice, Ministry of Interior (especially Violence Against Women Units), Ministry of Health, Ministry of Women, Family and Children, Ministry of Social Affairs, members of Parliament, High Judicial Council, National Commission of Bailiffs, Ordre National Des Avocats de Tunisie, International Association of Women Judges, and local civil society organizations. Indirect beneficiaries: Women and girls in Tunisia.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aimed to improve access to justice for women and girls, especially survivors of gender-based violence (GBV).</narrative>
  </description>
